{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789466473,"nonce":"Ijpz6eNrtx0SZvHZ/7qup/Ki6tA=","message":"Second finding: compiled code never counts callee invocations (beads tick only on host calls through the adapter; tier-0 code asks for promotion at entry when it holds an OSR-eligible loop). A probe with a non-tail-recursive struct-passing callee called 400M times from main's loop promoted nothing in 2.5 s under tiered-llvm: main's loop has a call so no layout, the callee is never counted. Today callees reach LLVM only inside a root's closure. With own-module promotion the root's direct callees must be queued after it (breadth-first, each through the headroom gate dbe58cb, each bound through its cell so the root picks it up), or tier-0 entries must count. Ash ticks beads from its interpreter and re-tiers loops by polls; Zyntax's tiered profile has no interpreter, so the queued-callee ladder is the fit.","files":null}]}